public class Solution {

    /**
     * Performs modular exponentiation to compute (g^n) % p.
     * Uses the binary exponentiation algorithm to maintain efficiency
     * and prevent overflow issues.
     */
    public int modp(int g, int n, int p) {
        long base = g % p;
        long result = 1;
        long exp = n;

        while (exp > 0) {
            // If exponent is odd, multiply result with base
            if (exp % 2 == 1) {
                result = (result * base) % p;
            }
            // Exponent must be even now
            exp = exp >> 1; // divide by 2
            base = (base * base) % p;
        }
        return (int) result;
    }
}
```

The logic behind the `modp` method is based on the property of modular arithmetic that states (a * b) % p = ((a % p) * (b % p)) % p. By squaring the base at each step and multiplying the result only when the current bit of the exponent is set, we decompose the operation into a series of smaller, manageable steps. This prevents the value of g^n from ever exceeding the limits of standard computer memory, as the modulo operator is applied at every multiplication.

